      "side": 1,
      "message": "The callback goes through matchCallback in sdbusplus/bus/match.cpp. That function is noexcept and calls (*c)(message) with no try/catch. If unpack\u003c\u003e throws, std::terminate is called, so the guard does fix a real crash.\n\nThe match rule is interfacesAdded on /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory. The signal is emitted by the ObjectMapper or the inventory manager with a fixed D-Bus signature (oa{sa{sv}}).\n\nA malformed signal is unlikely. The more probable cause is a variant type mismatch. InterfaceMap only allows bool, std::string and int64_t. Real inventory objects carry double, uint32_t, arrays and so on, so unpack throws for them.\n\nThis is an existing problem and a likely functional bug: any new inventory object with other property types makes the unpack throw. The catch now swallows that, and the chassis is silently never detected.\n\nBefore this patch, such a signal would have crashed the daemon, which is arguably better for finding the problem. A more robust fix is to unpack only sdbusplus::object_path and the interface names, using std::map\u003cstd::string, std::map\u003cstd::string, std::variant\u003c...\u003e\u003e\u003e with a wider variant or std::monostate-style skipping. Alternatively, read oa{sa{sv}} and ignore the values.\n\nThis change hides the problem behind an error log. Was the unpack failure actually observed?\n\nsetupMonitoringForChassis() and checkAvailability() are now inside the same try block. setupMonitoringForChassis() can throw SdBusError from sdbusplus::match construction. The catch handles that, but it leaves partial state behind. discoveredChassisNumbers.insert() has already run, so the chassis is never retried, and chassisStates and propertyMatches may be half-populated.\n\nSuggestion: insert into discoveredChassisNumbers only after setupMonitoringForChassis() succeeds, or roll back in the catch.\n\n[ifaces \u003d interfaces] copies the whole map on each lambda construction. This is pre-existing. A capture by reference would be cheaper.",
